commercial real estate, real estate construction and development, residential real estate, small business administration, consumer, and other loan products. In addition, it offers treasury management and international trade services; life insurance premium and sponsor finance; tax credit related lending; tax credit brokerage services; other deposit accounts, such as community associations, property management, legal industry and escrow services; treasury management product and services; customized solutions and products; cash management systems; fiduciary, investment management, and financial advisory services; and customer hedging products, international banking, card services, and tax credit businesses. Further, the company provides online, device applications, text, and voice banking; remote deposit capture; internet banking, mobile banking, cash management, positive pay, fraud detection and prevention, automated payables, check image, and statement and document imaging services; and controlled disbursements, repurchase agreements, and sweep investment accounts. Financial Services Corp was founded in 1988 and is headquartered in Clayton, Missouri.","ceo_bio":"Mr. James Brian Lally | President, CEO & Director | Age 57 | Compensation: $2.0M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 1K people. Revenue per employee of $540K ranks in the above the sector median among Financial Services peers, with revenue growing 13%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $133K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $10K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 4\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (3/10).","financial_summary":["Enterprise Financial Services C is a mid-cap financial services business with a $2.2B market cap on $740M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +12% over the past three years and +18% annually across the full decade, a respectable pace for a business of this size.","Returns on capital run at 12% against a 8% cost of capital, a 4-point spread that creates value, though narrow enough that competitive pressure could close it.","The business throws off $182M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 25 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, with adequate 0.96\u00d7 cash conversion.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.0\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and thin 1.1\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield of 2.7% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (0.3) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Piotroski score 4/9 \u2014 below the median for a healthy business","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Credit cycles and regulatory capital \u2014 losses accumulate invisibly in good years and appear all at once, and the time to worry is when underwriting standards are loosest.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Fintech disintermediation \u2014 neobanks, embedded finance, and blockchain rails are unbundling the fee streams that traditional banks took for granted.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Interest rate regime shifts \u2014 a rapid move in rates can invert the yield curve, crush bond portfolios, and trigger deposit flight simultaneously.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Operating margins 9% above sector median \u2014 the quantitative trace of pricing power","Trading at a 41% margin of safety to estimated fair value \u2014 a real cushion against errors in the estimate","Market is pricing in lower growth than the company has historically delivered","21 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment","Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment risk"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"How (EFSC) Movements Inform Risk Allocation Models","source":"news.stocktradersdaily.com","link":"https://news.stocktradersdaily.com/news_release/89/How_EFSC_Movements_Inform_Risk_Allocation_Models_111026103402_1794368042.html","date":"2026-11-11","timestamp":1794368042.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":-0.494,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Enterprise Financial Services (EFSC) Stock May Be 41% Below Fair Value","source":"simplywall.st","link":"https://simplywall.st/stocks/us/banks/nasdaq-efsc/enterprise-financial-services/news/enterprise-financial-services-efsc-stock-may-be-41-below-fai","date":"2026-11-10","timestamp":1794306043.211,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.925,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Is Enterprise Financial Services (EFSC) Undervalued As Credit Cleanup Progress Shapes Its Valuation?","source":"simplywall.st","link":"https://simplywall.st/stocks/us/banks/nasdaq-efsc/enterprise-financial-services/news/is-enterprise-financial-services-efsc-undervalued-as-credit","date":"2026-11-09","timestamp":1794212963.918,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.94,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Learn Why The Bull Case For Enterprise Financial Services (EFSC) Could Change Following Higher Credit Losses","source":"simplywall.st","link":"https://simplywall.st/stocks/us/banks/nasdaq-efsc/enterprise-financial-services/news/learn-why-the-bull-case-for-enterprise-financial-services-ef","date":"2026-11-03","